# Agent guide: `{{ project_name }}`

Guidance for AI coding assistants working on this project.
`CLAUDE.md` loads this file via the `@AGENTS.md` import.

## Project

- Package: `{{ package_name }}/`
- Python:  {{ python_version }}+
- Kind:    {% if is_library %}distributable library (`uv_build` backend){% else %}application, not packaged for distribution{% endif %}

## Toolchain

The Astral toolchain. Keep it unless there is a clear reason to change:

- `uv`: environment, locking, running
- `ruff`: linting + formatting
- `ty`: type checking
{%- if use_pytest %}
- `pytest`: testing
- `pytest-cov`: coverage measurement and threshold gate
{%- endif %}

## Commands

```bash
uv sync --group dev      # create/refresh the environment
uv run ruff check .      # lint
uv run ruff format .     # format
uv run ty check          # type-check
{%- if use_pytest %}
uv run pytest            # run tests (coverage included by default)
uv run pytest --no-cov   # skip coverage for quick iteration
uv run pytest -m "not slow"  # skip tests marked slow
{%- endif %}
```
{% if use_pytest %}
Coverage is measured by `pytest-cov` against `{{ package_name }}/` with
branch tracking; the `fail_under` gate lives in `pyproject.toml`
(see *Tests → Coverage and speed*).
{% endif %}

## Workflow

A change is done when the gates pass. Before reporting it finished,
run them in this order and fix what they find:

```bash
uv run ruff format .
uv run ruff check --fix .
uv run ty check
{%- if use_pytest %}
uv run pytest
{%- else %}
uv run python -c "import {{ package_name }}"
{%- endif %}
```

If one cannot run here (a test needing hardware or a service this
machine lacks), say which and why instead of skipping it silently. CI
runs the same gates, but it should confirm a change, not discover that
it is broken.
{%- if is_library %}

CI also builds the sdist and wheel, checks their metadata, and imports
the package from the wheel alone. When a change touches packaging
(`pyproject.toml`, the package layout, or files the package ships), run
`uv build` and `uvx twine check dist/*` locally too.
{%- endif %}

- Commit only when the user asks; see *Committing* below.
- Push only when the user asks; a release the user asked for includes
  its push and tag. Never force-push, rewrite pushed history, or move
  or delete a tag.
- Work on the current branch, usually `main`. Create a branch only when
  the user asks for one.
- Never skip git hooks (`--no-verify`).

### Committing

Changes accumulate in the working tree until the user asks for a
commit. Then split them into single-purpose commits that follow the
*Commit convention* below, one logical unit at a time, with
`git stash`:

1. Set every other unit aside:
   `git stash push --include-untracked -m "<units>" -- <their paths>`.
2. Run the gates on what remains, so each commit passes on its own,
   and commit it.
3. `git stash pop`, and repeat until `git stash list` is empty.

A file holding parts of two units goes into a stash of its own, pushed
last so it is `stash@{0}`. Bring it back with
`git checkout stash@{0} -- <file>`, remove the other unit's part,
stage, and commit; then run `git checkout stash@{0} -- <file>` again
to restore the rest, and `git stash drop` that stash.

- Order the units so each commit builds only on the ones before it.
- No `git add -p` or other interactive commands.
- Drop a stash only once its changes are back in the working tree.
- Report the commits made, one line each.

### Secrets

- No token, password, or private key in code, docs, commit messages,
  or logs, nor a test or example value that is real somewhere.
- Code reads a secret from an environment variable. `.env` holds the
  local values and is never committed (`.gitignore` covers it); the
  README's {% if is_library %}📦 Installation{% else %}📦 Setup{% endif %} names each variable the project
  needs, never its value.
- If a secret has been committed, stop and tell the user before
  changing anything: removing it from history does not take it back.
  The credential has to be revoked and replaced.

## Commit convention

Commit messages are written in English. A message is one subject line,
then optionally a blank line and a body, then the trailers:

```
<gitmoji> <Verb> <object> [<preposition> <target>]

<Optional body: why, wrapped at 72 columns>

Co-Authored-By: <agent name> <agent email>
```

A commit holds one change together with the tests, docs, and
`CHANGELOG.md` entry that come with it. Those take the change's emoji,
not one of their own.

### Subject

- **Emoji**: one from the table below, written exactly as it appears
  there. Some carry an invisible variation selector, and a different
  byte form slips past `git log --grep`. Look one up on
  [gitmoji.dev](https://gitmoji.dev/) only when none here fits, and
  never use an emoji that is not a Gitmoji. 💥 takes precedence over
  any other emoji when the change breaks something users rely on.
- **Verb**: a capitalized imperative, preferably the one the table
  pairs with the emoji. Refactor, Update, Change, and Improve only
  repeat the emoji, so use them only when no concrete verb fits. Never
  a figurative verb (Say, Give, Let, Keep).
- **Object**: the name of what changed, in backticks: a file, a module
  path, a package, or an identifier (`filesystem.utils`,
  `pyproject.toml`, `ruff`, `StagedFile.commit`). Leave out the
  top-level package (`filesystem.utils`, not
  `{{ package_name }}.filesystem.utils`). Names that changed together
  share a subject (`a`, `b`, and `c`); beyond a few, name their common
  module. Add a kind word (class, function) only when the name alone is
  ambiguous, and write no `()` after a function name.
- **Versions** are plain (0.17.0); a **tag** takes backticks
  (`v2.0.0`).
- **Length**: aim for about 50 characters and never pass 72. No
  trailing period.
- **One change**: two unrelated changes joined by "and" are two
  commits. Names listed with "and" are fine.

### Body

Optional, but written for a 💥 commit, where it gives the migration,
and whenever another rule sends something to the commit message: the
cause of a bug, or a measurement and the conditions it was taken
under.{% if include_todo %} The same goes for why a `TODO.md` item was
dropped.{% endif %} The body says why, since the diff already shows
what. Wrap it at 72 columns and list several points as `-` bullets.

### Trailers

AI assistants end the message with `Co-Authored-By:` and their own
published identity, e.g. `Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>`.

### Emoji

| Emoji | For | Verbs |
| ----- | --- | ----- |
| ✨ | a new feature | Add |
| 🐛 | a bug fix, urgent or not | Fix |
| 🔒️ | a security or privacy fix | Fix |
| 💥 | a breaking change | any verb in this table |
| ♻️ | restructuring without a behavior change | Split, Extract, Inline, Combine, Rename, Simplify |
| ⚡️ | performance | Speed up, Cache |
| 🎨 | formatting or code layout | Format, Reorder |
| 🔥 | removing code or files, dead code included | Remove |
| 🗑️ | a deprecation | Deprecate |
| 🏷️ | type annotations | Annotate |
| 💬 | text and literals: messages, strings | Reword |
| 💡 | source comments | Add, Reword |
| 📝 | documentation | Document, Add, Correct |
| ✏️ | a typo | Fix |
| ✅ | tests | Add tests for, Fix tests for |
| 🚚 | moving or renaming files and modules | Move, Rename |
| 🔧 | configuration | Configure, Enable, Disable, Set |
| 👷 | CI | Add, Configure |
| ⬆️ | upgrading a dependency or the template | Upgrade, Update |
| ➕ | adding a dependency | Add |
| ➖ | removing a dependency | Remove |
| 🙈 | `.gitignore` | Ignore |
| 📄 | the license | Update |
| 🚧 | unfinished work the user asked to commit | any |
| 🔖 | a release | Release |
| ⏪️ | a revert | Revert |
| 🎉 | the first commit | a fixed form |
🚧 is only for unfinished work the user asks to commit; never commit
work in progress on your own.

### Fixed forms

```
⬆️ Upgrade `ruff` to 0.17.0
⬆️ Upgrade `ruff`, `ty`, and `uv_build`
➕ Add `pytest-order` 1.2.0 to dev dependencies
➖ Remove `pytest-order`
⬆️ Update the copier template to `v2.0.0`
🔖 Release version 2.0.0
⏪️ Revert "<original subject>"
🎉 Initial commit from copier template
```

An upgrade of several packages lists their versions in the body; a
revert's body is `This reverts commit <hash>.`, as `git revert` writes
it.

### Examples

| Instead of | Write |
| ---------- | ----- |
| `✨ Add filesystem utility functions` | `` ✨ Add `ensure_dir` and `make_dirs` to `filesystem.utils` `` |
| `` ♻️ Refactor `filesystem.directory` `` | `` ♻️ Split `existence` out of `filesystem.directory` `` |
| `⚡️ Let an estimator make its algorithm on the first flow asked of it` | `⚡️ Build the estimator's algorithm on its first flow` |
| `✏️ Say what a writer wrote, and take a measurement out of a docstring` | Two commits: `` 💬 Reword `FrameWriter.report` `` and `` 📝 Remove a measurement from `identity_ssim` `` |
## Code style

`ruff` and `ty` check much of this, so run `uv run ruff format` and
`uv run ruff check --fix` rather than applying it by hand; the rest is
written here. *All Python code* covers tests as well; *Source code*
does not{% if use_pytest %}, and *Tests* says what tests do differently{% endif %}.

### All Python code

A module runs, top to bottom:

1. `from __future__ import annotations` (ruff `required-imports`),
   except in an `__init__.py`, which does not need it.
2. `__all__` as a tuple, where the module has one; in an `__init__.py`
   it comes before the re-exports.
3. Imports in three groups: standard library, third party, then this
   project. Within a group `import X` precedes `from X import Y`, and
   names are alphabetical (ruff `I`).
4. An optional `if TYPE_CHECKING:` block with the type-only imports,
   grouped the same way.
5. The module body: constants, type aliases, functions, and classes.
6. An `if __name__ == "__main__":` guard, in a runnable script only.

- One blank line separates the parts above, and the import groups.
  After the import section (the `TYPE_CHECKING` block included), two
  blank lines come before a function or a class and one before a
  constant or an alias, as ruff's import sorting (`I001`) expects. Two
  blank lines separate top-level definitions; one separates methods.
- Import by absolute path. A relative import is for the rare case an
  absolute one cannot work, and carries `# noqa: TID252` with the
  reason.
- Define a private helper above its first caller, so the module reads
  top-down. Private and public definitions are not kept apart.
- Keep every line within 88 columns, docstrings, comments, and strings
  included (ruff `E501`). `ruff format` wraps code; shorten or rewrap
  the rest.
- A call that does not fit on one line takes one argument per line,
  with a trailing comma so the formatter keeps that shape. Assign a
  setup call to a local first rather than nesting it in another call's
  arguments.
- An import or an `__all__` that fits on one line stays on one line:
  leave no trailing comma that keeps it split.
- A comprehension takes one `for`. Let `ruff format` break one that
  does not fit; write a loop for anything more.
- In a function body, a blank line separates logical groups; a body of
  one or two lines has none.
- Use builtin generics (`list`, `dict`, `type`), `X | None` unions, and
  PEP 695 syntax (`type Alias = ...`, `def f[T](...)`, `class C[T]:`),
  never `TypeVar`, `TypeAlias`, `Optional`, or `Union`.
- Use `pathlib`, not `os.path` (ruff `PTH`).
- Fix a `ruff` or `ty` finding rather than suppressing it. When a
  suppression is right, name the code and say why in a comment:
  `# noqa: <code>`, or `# ty: ignore[<error-name>]` with `ty`'s own
  error names (`invalid-argument-type`), never a bare one.
- In a long module, group related definitions under a boxed banner: a
  centred title-case title between two `#`-bordered rules, 30 columns
  wide, with two blank lines on each side:

  ```python
  # ========================== #
  #          Reading           #
  # ========================== #
  ```

- Prose in docstrings, comments, and messages uses no em dash and no
  ` -- `. Recast the sentence: a colon, a comma, parentheses, or two
  sentences.
- In docstrings and comments, single backticks mark identifiers,
  values, and expressions alike (`path`, `"merge"`, `(H, W)`); use
  double backticks only when the content holds a backtick.
- Standalone runnable scripts carry PEP 723 inline metadata (the
  `# /// script` block). `uv` manages it (`uv add --script`); add or
  edit it by hand only when explicitly asked.

### Source code

Modules and classes:

- A module takes no docstring unless the user asks for one.
- A module-level constant is `UPPER_SNAKE` and annotated `Final`.
- A class body opens with its docstring, then `__slots__` and class
  attributes, then `__init__` (or `__post_init__`) and `__repr__`;
  `__enter__` and `__exit__` close it. Order the methods between for
  readability and consistency: related methods together, and sibling
  classes in the same order.
- Mark every override with `@override`, an implemented
  `abstractmethod` included. A self-evident dunder (`__len__`,
  `__str__`) needs none.
- An `@abstractmethod` takes a docstring and a bare
  `raise NotImplementedError`; a `Protocol` member keeps `...`.
- A value type is `@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)`; a plain
  `@dataclass` is for mutable configuration.
- A closed set of options is a `Literal` alias rather than an `Enum`.

Typing:

- Keep code fully typed: `ty` runs with `error-on-warning`.
- `ty` has no plugin system; rely on standard typing (PEP 681
  `dataclass_transform`, `.pyi` stubs), not type-checker plugins.

Errors and messages:

- Raise built-in exceptions. Add a custom one only when callers need to
  catch it apart; name it `*Error` and subclass one built-in.
- Build the message in a `msg` variable (ruff `EM`), on one line:
  `msg = f"..."`. When it would run past 88 columns, shorten the prose,
  hoist a value to a local, or use `{value=}`; never split the string.
- A message starts lower-case and ends without a period; one that
  begins with an identifier keeps its case. Show a value with `!r`
  rather than hand-written quotes, and put a path at the end.
- Name the valid set or the fix, not just the failure ("unsupported
  node type 'X': expected File, Directory, ..."; "balance pause/resume,
  or use `suspend()`"), so the message tells the caller what to do
  next.
- Log with `%`-style arguments, not an f-string (ruff `G`).

Docstrings and comments:

- Docstrings are optional{% if use_pytest %} (for tests, see *Tests*){% endif %}: write them where
  they clarify intent, not mechanically. "Mechanically" targets two
  habits to avoid: comments (or docstrings) that merely restate the
  code, and a base class whose docstring explains itself in terms of its
  specific subclasses (except a *closed* hierarchy's base, which may
  name its subclasses as a deliberate family map). It is *not* a licence
  to leave a consumed method bare. When written, document
  *intent and contracts, not mechanism*:
  - Lead with a one-line summary ending in a period: a declarative noun
    phrase for classes ("An ordered, read-only view over ..."), an
    imperative verb phrase for functions and methods ("Yield successive
    windows from `items`."). Two kinds take a noun phrase instead: a
    property getter ("The reporting unit ...") and a boolean-returning
    *method*, which leads with "Whether ..." ("Whether `path`
    exists."). A boolean *function* stays imperative ("Test whether a
    path exists.").
  - A concrete public method a caller consumes must be self-explainable
    from its own docstring and signature; never lean on an inherited
    parent docstring. Abstract base methods document only the generic
    contract and never name a specific subclass.
  - Surface what callers cannot infer from the signature alone:
    invariants, edge cases, what subclasses must override, policy
    trade-offs. Skip restating what the code already shows.
  - Use [Google style](https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#38-comments-and-docstrings)
    sections (`Args:`, `Returns:`, `Yields:`, `Raises:`,
    `Type Parameters:`); omit types from `Args:` since the signature
    already carries them. Custom sections (`Example:`, `Truth table:`,
    and ad-hoc labels) are welcome when they clarify a real pitfall or
    pattern.
  - Add an `Args:` / `Returns:` block only for what the summary and
    signature cannot already convey. When they make the behaviour
    obvious (a no-arg getter, a self-evident one-liner), the summary
    *is* the whole docstring. Document an edge case shared across a
    family once on the class.
  - Document a constructor's arguments in the class docstring's
    `Args:` and leave `__init__` bare. An `@overload` stub takes no
    docstring; the implementation carries it.
  - Reference identifiers in backticks (`my_method`, `param`,
    `MyClass.method`). Literal option values get backticks too
    (`"merge"`, `"error"`), as identifiers do.
- Comments explain why, not what: a constraint, a reason, a rejected
  alternative. Keep a comment to one line, three at most; anything
  longer belongs in a docstring or a commit message. An inline comment
  is for a short qualifier, such as a unit.
- Fill docstring and comment prose toward 88 columns: a line was
  wrapped early if the next line's first word would still have fit.
  Nothing checks this (`E501` catches only overflow), so rewrap and
  read the result. Never split a backticked span or an `*emphasis*`
  across lines.

{%- if use_pytest %}

## Tests

### Layout

- Tests live in `tests/` and may use bare `assert` (ruff `S101` is
  waived there).
- Mirror the package layout under `tests/`, one `test_<module>.py` per
  source module: `{{ package_name }}/sub/mod.py` is tested by
  `tests/sub/test_mod.py`. Keep `__init__.py` markers in test
  subpackages (matches the `INP` ruff rule).
- Not every source file needs a dedicated test file: types-only
  modules, re-export `__init__.py` markers, and details covered through
  a public-facing module are intentional exceptions.
- Fixtures live in `tests/conftest.py` when you need them (modern
  default, applies to `tests/` only). Use a root `conftest.py` only for
  `pytest_plugins` declarations, doctest fixtures shared with source
  files, or project-wide collection hooks.
- Code shared across tests lives in a `fixtures/` package: either
  `tests/fixtures/`, whose modules the root `conftest.py` loads through
  `pytest_plugins`, or `tests/<sub>/fixtures/` for one subpackage,
  imported by that subpackage's `conftest.py` so it applies below it
  only (pytest honors `pytest_plugins` in the root `conftest.py`
  alone).
- Add a `conftest.py` or a `helpers.py` wherever tests need one:
  per-package configuration in `conftest.py`, plain helper functions
  and classes in `tests/<pkg>/helpers.py`. Shared skip marks go in
  `tests/marks.py`.
- Import the code under test the way its users do, from its public
  path. Import a helper by its absolute path,
  `from tests.<pkg>.helpers import ...`: `tests/` is a package, so this
  works under `--import-mode=importlib`.
- Type annotations are optional in tests: `ruff`'s `ANN` rules are
  waived for `tests/**`, and `ty` does not check `tests/`.

### Test data

- Data files the tests read live in `tests/assets/`, which is not a
  Python package and mirrors nothing. If the project has a module named
  `assets`, choose another name and record it below the marker.
- A test that writes files does so under pytest's `tmp_path` (or
  `tmp_path_factory` for data shared by a module), never in the
  working directory or a fixed path.
- Prefer data a test builds in `tmp_path` over a committed file. Commit
  a file only when building it would hide what the test checks: a real
  file header, a sample from a device.
- Data too large or private to commit goes in `tests/assets/local/`,
  which `.gitignore` excludes, and is opt-in: its fixture is
  parametrized over the files present, so a clone without them skips
  those tests instead of failing.

### Writing tests

- Flat module-level `def test_*` functions by default; reach for a
  plain `class TestX:` (grouping only, no inheritance) to organize a
  large or multi-feature surface. Don't mix the two styles within one
  file.
- Name a test for the behavior it checks, as a phrase:
  `test_folder_error_ends_with_the_offending_full_path`.
- Add no docstrings or comments to test functions and classes; the
  name says what a test checks. Keep the comments already there, and
  document an existing test only when the user asks for it. A helper,
  fixture, or fake that several tests share may take a short
  docstring.
- When a parametrized case's values do not say what it covers, name
  it with `ids=` or `pytest.param(..., id="empty-source")`, so a
  failure reads `test_load[empty-source]` rather than
  `test_load[3-True]`.
- Tests are independent of each other and of their order. A test that
  changes module-level state (a registry, a cache, an environment
  variable) restores it: `monkeypatch` for an attribute or a variable,
  an autouse fixture in that package's `conftest.py` for state a whole
  package touches. Fixtures keep the default function scope; widen it
  only for setup that is expensive and never mutated.
- Seed randomness per test with a local generator
  (`random.Random(<seed>)`, `numpy.random.default_rng(<seed>)`), never a
  global seed. Compare floats with `pytest.approx`, or
  `numpy.testing.assert_allclose` for arrays, giving the tolerance the
  check needs rather than the default.
- Test through the public API. Reach into a private name (`_name`) only
  when it is itself the contract under test, with `# noqa: SLF001` on
  that line.
- Tests never touch the network or an outside service: fake the
  boundary with `monkeypatch`.
- Assertions check concrete return values *and* side effects, not
  merely "no exception raised"; error paths use
  `pytest.raises(..., match=...)`; verify numbers against independently
  computed values, not the implementation's own output; make timing and
  IO deterministic (an injected clock, fault injection) rather than
  flaky. A good test fails on *subtle* breakage, not just obvious
  breakage. When a contract is "one batched call per source",
  verify it with a spy, not only by the result.
- A warning the code emits on purpose is behavior: assert it with
  `pytest.warns(Category, match=...)`. Test log output where it is the
  product (a run's report, a message a user acts on) through
  `caplog.records`: the level and `record.getMessage()`, not the
  formatted `caplog.text`.
- Replace or observe a collaborator with `monkeypatch`, not
  `unittest.mock`: patch the attribute with a small fake, or wrap the
  real method in a spy that records each call and delegates to it. A
  spy keeps the real behavior, so the test checks the contract rather
  than a scripted double. Patch the name where the code looks it up; a
  fixture that patches returns what it records.
- A bug fix starts with a test that fails on the bug. Fix the code and
  keep the test as the guard, named for the behavior it pins down, not
  for the bug report.

### Skipped tests

- A test that cannot run everywhere is skipped (never deleted or left
  failing), and its `reason` names what is missing ("needs a CUDA
  device", "symlinks need privilege on Windows"). Pick the mechanism by
  what is missing:
  - an optional dependency: `pytest.importorskip("<module>")` at the
    top of the module;
  - a platform, device, or service: a `skipif` mark defined once in
    `tests/marks.py` and imported where needed
    (`from tests.marks import requires_cuda`), never redefined per
    file;
  - data the repository does not hold: the opt-in fixture of
    *Test data*.
- Parametrize over the condition rather than duplicating a test
  (`pytest.param("cuda", marks=requires_cuda)`), so a machine without
  it still runs the other cases.
- When CI cannot run the tests (hardware its runners lack), say so
  below the marker in this file. The local run is then the gate: run
  the full suite on a machine that has the hardware before asking for a
  commit, and report any group that skipped.

### Coverage and speed

- With `fail_under = 0` coverage is measured, not enforced. Once the
  tests cover the {% if is_library %}public API{% else %}main code paths{% endif %}, set it a few points below the
  measured total and raise it as coverage grows; the libraries built
  from this template hold 95.
- Never lower `fail_under` to let a change pass: cover the change, or
  exclude a line that cannot run under test with `# pragma: no cover`
  and a comment saying why. Leave out only modules with nothing to
  execute, such as type aliases, through `omit` in
  `[tool.coverage.run]`.
- Keep the suite fast enough to run before every commit. A test that
  takes seconds gets `@pytest.mark.slow`, registered in
  `pyproject.toml`; `uv run pytest -m "not slow"` serves quick
  iteration, and the full suite still runs before a commit and in CI.
{%- endif %}

## Dependencies

- Change dependencies with `uv`, not by editing `pyproject.toml` by
  hand: `uv add <pkg>` / `uv remove <pkg>` for what the code imports at
  run time, `uv add --group dev <pkg>` for what only tests, linting, or
  type checking need{% if is_library %}, and `uv add --optional <extra> <pkg>` for
  an optional extra{% endif %}. Commit `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock`
  together: CI installs with `uv sync --locked` and fails on a stale
  lock.
- To move to a newer release, raise the minimum with
  `uv add "<pkg>>=X.Y.Z"` (keep the `--group`{% if is_library %} or `--optional`{% endif %} flag
  it was added with), or refresh only the lock with
  `uv lock --upgrade-package <pkg>`.
- Raising `ruff`'s minimum also raises `[tool.ruff] required-version`
  and the "omitted (ruff vX.Y.Z)" note at the end of `pyproject.toml`;
  re-check that the sections it lists still use only defaults.
- A runtime dependency's range is a requirement users see, so it goes
  in `CHANGELOG.md`; `dev` group changes stay out (see
  *Documentation → `CHANGELOG.md`*).

### Supported Python versions

The supported versions are stated in several places. Change them
together, in one commit:

- `requires-python` in `pyproject.toml`: the oldest supported version;
- `[tool.ty.environment] python-version`: the same oldest version, so
  `ty` flags syntax and APIs newer than it;
- one `Programming Language :: Python :: X.Y` classifier per supported
  version;
- the `python-version` list in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`: every
  supported version, each tested on Linux;
- `.python-version`: the version development, and CI on Windows and
  macOS, run on (any supported one);
- the README's Python badge, which lists every supported version, and
  the requirement line in {% if is_library %}📦 Installation{% else %}📦 Setup{% endif %}.

The range comes from the template: the oldest version is the
`python_version` answer, and the newest is the newest version the
template offers. `copier update -d python_version=X.Y` moves the oldest
and re-renders all of the above and the *Project* line of this file;
a plain `copier update` after the template offers a newer Python
extends the range.

To leave a version out (a dependency has no wheels for it yet), drop
it from the classifiers, the CI list, and the badge together, and add
it back once the dependency catches up. Dropping a version a release
has supported is a `**Breaking:**` `Changed` entry in `CHANGELOG.md`;
supporting a new one is an `Added` entry.

## Documentation

`README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`,{% if include_todo %} `TODO.md`,{% endif %} and this
file are kept current by whoever changes the code: update them in the
same commit as the change they describe, not as a follow-up. A
docs-only fix (wording, a stale example) is its own `📝` commit.

| When a change ...                                         | Update                                   |
| --------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| alters user-visible behavior, requirements, or dependencies | `CHANGELOG.md` → `[Unreleased]`        |
{%- if is_library %}
| adds, removes, or renames a public module                 | `README.md` → 🧩 Modules                 |
| adds, removes, or changes a command in `[project.scripts]` | `README.md` → ⌨️ CLI                     |
| changes how the package is installed                      | `README.md` → 📦 Installation            |
{%- else %}
| changes how the project is set up                         | `README.md` → 📦 Setup                   |
| changes how the project is run                            | `README.md` → 🚀 Usage                   |
| adds, moves, or removes a top-level directory             | `README.md` → 🗂️ Layout, if present      |
{%- endif %}
| changes code that a README example uses                   | that README → the example                |
| moves, renames, or deletes a file a README links to       | that README → the link                   |
| adds, removes, renames, or reorders a sub-README section  | that README → `## Contents`, if present  |
{%- if include_todo %}
| defers work, or uncovers a problem it does not fix        | `TODO.md` → add an item                  |
| resolves a `TODO.md` item                                 | `TODO.md` → remove the item              |
{%- endif %}
| settles a design choice later work should not reopen      | `docs/decisions/` → add a record         |
| settles a convention later sessions must follow           | `AGENTS.md` → below the marker           |

Each document has one audience and one tense:

- `README.md` describes the project **as it is now**: what it does and
  how to {% if is_library %}install and use it{% else %}set it up and run it{% endif %}. No history, no "new in vX.Y".
- `CHANGELOG.md` records what **changed for a user** of the
  {% if is_library %}library{% else %}application{% endif %}: external behavior only, never internal refactoring.
{%- if include_todo %}
- `TODO.md` lists work that is **not done yet**.
{%- endif %}
- `docs/decisions/` records **why** the code is shaped as it is: one
  decision per file, written once.
- `AGENTS.md` holds rules for whoever works on the code next.

All of them wrap prose at 72 columns. Tables, code blocks, headings,
and a line holding a long URL or command are exempt. Write a new
document in English; a document already written in another language
keeps that language. No document uses an em dash or ` -- ` in its
prose: recast the sentence with a colon, a comma, parentheses, or two
sentences.
{%- if not include_todo %}

This project keeps no `TODO.md`: report work you defer to the user.
`ruff` rejects a `# TODO` comment (its `FIX` rules), so one cannot
hold it either.
{%- endif %}

### `CHANGELOG.md`

The format is [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/).

- New entries go under `## [Unreleased]`, grouped into these six
  categories and no others, always in this order:

  | Category     | For                                          |
  | ------------ | -------------------------------------------- |
  | `Added`      | new features                                 |
  | `Changed`    | changes in existing functionality            |
  | `Deprecated` | features that will be removed                |
  | `Removed`    | features removed now                         |
  | `Fixed`      | bug fixes                                    |
  | `Security`   | vulnerabilities                              |

  Omit empty categories and never invent new ones: a performance
  improvement is `Changed`, a fixed performance regression is `Fixed`.
  The first entry replaces the `_No entries yet._` placeholder.
- Record only changes to **external behavior** (what someone who
  {% if is_library %}imports the library{% else %}runs the application{% endif %} can observe without reading its source):
{%- if is_library %}
  - the public API: importable names without a leading `_`, their
    signatures, return values, raised exceptions, and warnings;
  - results, side effects, and file or serialization formats;
{%- else %}
  - commands, command-line options, and configuration keys;
  - accepted inputs, produced outputs (files, formats, reports), exit
    codes, and results;
{%- endif %}
  - performance;
  - requirements: supported Python versions and runtime dependency
    ranges.

  Internal refactoring stays out: restructuring, renaming or moving
  {% if is_library %}private{% else %}internal{% endif %} code, removing dead code, tests, CI, dev-tooling and
  dev-dependency bumps, comments, docstrings, and the other Markdown
  documents. The test: would a user of the {% if is_library %}library{% else %}application{% endif %}
  notice the difference, or have to act on it? If a refactor does
  change behavior, however slightly, record the behavior change, not
  the refactor.
- One bullet per change, phrased to read on from its category heading:
  - `Added` and `Removed` lead with *what* as a noun phrase:
    "`CommitRetry`, a frozen retry policy for commit steps refused by a
    transient Windows lock." / "`utils.types`, replaced by PEP 695 type
    parameters."
  - `Changed`, `Deprecated`, `Fixed`, and `Security` make what the user
    sees the subject and describe the new state in the present tense:
    "`load()` now raises `FormatError` instead of `ValueError` on a
    malformed header." / "`StagedFile.commit` no longer fails on a
    transient Windows `PermissionError`."
  - Never open with the heading's own verb ("Added ...", "Fixed ...")
    or an imperative ("Add ...", "Fix ..."); that is commit-message
    style.
- Keep an entry short by default: one or two sentences saying what
  changed. Causes, internal mechanisms, and measurements belong in the
  commit message, not here.
- `**Breaking:**`, `Deprecated`, and `Removed` entries spell out the
  upgrade instead, since that is where a reader has to act. Say what
  changed, what behaved differently before or still works unchanged,
  and what to do now (the replacement or the migration step). A
  `Deprecated` entry also says when the feature goes, if that is known.
- Start every incompatible change with `**Breaking:**`, spelled exactly
  so. It can appear under `Changed`, `Removed`, `Fixed` (a fix that
  breaks code relying on the old behavior), and `Security`; `Added` and
  `Deprecated` never break anything. Pre-1.0, breaking changes ship in a
  minor bump.
{%- if is_library %}
- Before removing or renaming a public name, deprecate it when you can:
  keep it working for at least one release with a `DeprecationWarning`
  and list it under `Deprecated` with its replacement.
{%- else %}
- Before removing or renaming a command, an option, or a configuration
  key, deprecate it when you can: keep it working for at least one
  release with a warning and list it under `Deprecated` with its
  replacement.
{%- endif %}
  The eventual `Removed` entry is still `**Breaking:**` and says which
  version deprecated it ("deprecated since 0.15"). Something that has
  to go at once skips the `Deprecated` step but never the
  `**Breaking:**` mark.
- Backtick identifiers, option values, file names, and commands. Group
  several small changes to one feature as sub-bullets under a single
  **bold** lead-in. Wrap lines at 72 columns.
- `[Unreleased]` describes the net change since the last release. When
  an unreleased change is reworked, reverted, or fixed, edit or delete
  its entry instead of adding a new one: users never saw the
  intermediate state.
- Released sections are history: change them only to correct a factual
  error. A release pulled after publishing keeps its section, marked
  `## [X.Y.Z] - YYYY-MM-DD [YANKED]`.
- A changelog written before these rules keeps its released sections
  as they are. Bring only `[Unreleased]` in line, and add the link
  references for every past version.

Cut a release only when the user asks for one. You may suggest it when
`[Unreleased]` has grown long or holds a `**Breaking:**` change. To
promote `[Unreleased]` for release `X.Y.Z`:

1. Re-read the section: merge entries that describe one change and drop
   any whose subject came and went within the cycle.
2. Rename its heading to `## [X.Y.Z] - YYYY-MM-DD` (ISO 8601 date) and
   add a fresh, empty `## [Unreleased]` above it.
3. Update the link references at the bottom of the file:

   ```
   [Unreleased]: https://github.com/{{ github_username }}/{{ repo_name }}/compare/vX.Y.Z...HEAD
   [X.Y.Z]: https://github.com/{{ github_username }}/{{ repo_name }}/compare/vPREVIOUS...vX.Y.Z
   ```

   For the first release, `[X.Y.Z]` points to
   `https://github.com/{{ github_username }}/{{ repo_name }}/releases/tag/vX.Y.Z` instead.
{%- if include_todo %}

### `TODO.md`

`TODO.md` holds **open work only**: work deliberately deferred (out of
scope for the current change, blocked on a decision, or a limitation
found along the way). It is not a design document, a progress log, or a
record of what is done: decisions go to `docs/decisions/`, finished
work to git history and `CHANGELOG.md`.

- Before starting a task, read the items that touch the code it
  changes: one may already describe the problem, or be resolved by the
  change.
- An item is one bullet by default: what needs doing and why, led by
  the relevant path in backticks when there is one, as in
  "`io/reader.py`: stream large files instead of reading them whole
  (memory spikes above ~1 GB)."
- Only when one line cannot carry it, give the item a `### <what needs
  doing>` heading and a short body: the problem (a code snippet may show
  it), why it was deferred, and a closing `Revisit when ...` line naming
  what should reopen it: a use case that needs it, a measurement that
  would justify it, or the decision it waits on. A body that grows past
  a few short paragraphs is a design question: settle it in a
  `docs/decisions/` record and keep only the remaining work here.
- Items sit under `## Open`, most important first. Once the list
  outgrows a screen, replace `## Open` with `##` headings by area (a
  package or a topic), most important first within each. Headings
  carry no emoji, so their anchors stay plain.
- No owners, dates, status markers, checkboxes, or "last updated"
  lines: git history records who and when.
- Add an item only for work the current change does not finish. This
  file is the only place for it: `ruff` rejects `TODO`, `FIXME`,
  `XXX`, and `HACK` comments (its `FIX` rules), so deferred work
  cannot hide in the code.
- Delete an item in the commit that resolves it (no "Done" section, no
  strikethrough), and add a `CHANGELOG.md` entry if users can see the
  result. An item dropped without being done is deleted the same way,
  with the reason in the commit message. Restore `_No entries yet._`
  when the list becomes empty.
- A `TODO.md` written before these rules is brought in line once: move
  settled decisions to `docs/decisions/`, delete finished and
  struck-through items, and keep the remaining work as items.
{%- endif %}

### `README.md`

The root `README.md` is the landing page: what the project is, how to
{% if is_library %}install and use it{% else %}set it up and run it{% endif %}, and where to read further. It is not a
reference; signatures, options, and edge cases belong in docstrings.

Top to bottom, it holds:

1. The title (`name` in `pyproject.toml`) and the badges.
2. The one-line description in italics: word for word the
   `description` in `pyproject.toml`, trailing punctuation included,
   under 120 characters. Change the two together.
3. Optionally, a short paragraph with no heading: two to four
   sentences on scope, background, or who the project is for.
4. The lead sections, in this order:
{%- if is_library %}
   - 📦 Installation: the Python requirement, `uv add` / `pip install`,
     and each optional extra with what it adds.
   - 🚀 Quick start: one runnable example of the main use case.
     Examples for a single module belong in that module's README (see
     *Sub-READMEs*), not here.
   - 🧩 Modules: each public module or subpackage: its import path in
     backticks and what it offers, as a line, or as a short paragraph
     under a `###` heading once there is more to say.
{%- else %}
   - 📦 Setup: every step a fresh clone needs before the project runs:
     requirements (Python, hardware, drivers), `uv sync`, and one-time
     steps such as environment files or system libraries, each with its
     command.
   - 🚀 Usage: how to run the project: its commands, scripts, or entry
     points, and where their configuration lives.
   - 🗂️ Layout, once the tree no longer explains itself: one line per
     top-level directory a reader needs to find their way.
{%- endif %}

   The first entry in a section replaces its placeholder.
5. Project-specific sections, as the project needs them (a key
   concept, platform notes, acknowledgements), in the order a reader
   needs them, acknowledgements last.{% if is_library %} A command-line tool the
   library ships (`[project.scripts]`) comes first among them, as
   ⌨️ CLI: each command with one example.{% endif %}
6. The closing sections, always last and in this order:
   {% if include_todo %}📋 TODO, {% endif %}📜 Changelog, ⚖️ License.

- Every `##` heading starts with one emoji, as the template's do, and
  no two sections share one.
- Keep the template's sections, their names, and their order, and edit
  them in place; a project-specific section goes only where step 5
  puts it.
- No hand-written table of contents and no "back to top" links: GitHub
  gives every README an Outline button, and a root README long enough to
  need either is carrying material that belongs in a sub-README or in
  docstrings. Hand-made links to these headings also break silently: an
  emoji such as ⚖️ leaves an invisible U+FE0F in its anchor
  (`#️-license`){% if is_library %}, on GitHub and PyPI alike{% endif %}.
- Keep the Python requirement in the badge and in {% if is_library %}📦 Installation{% else %}📦 Setup{% endif %}
  in step with `requires-python` in `pyproject.toml`.
- A badge for a dependency (a framework, a CUDA version) goes right
  after the Python badge and shows the minimum in `pyproject.toml`; it
  moves with that minimum.
- Every code example runs against the current code: a change that breaks
  one updates it in the same commit. Show the result when it is the
  point, as a trailing `# -> ...` comment.
- Links stay valid: a commit that moves, renames, or deletes a file or
  directory fixes every README link to it: the root's hub links and
  the sub-READMEs' relative ones. `git grep -n '<old path>' -- '*.md'`
  finds them.
{%- if is_library %}
- The root README is also the PyPI project page, which has none of the
  repository's other files. Link a repository file by absolute URL
  (`https://github.com/{{ github_username }}/{{ repo_name }}/blob/main/<path>`, or
  `/tree/main/<dir>` for a directory), never by a relative path such as
  `./CHANGELOG.md`, which 404s there; in-page `#anchors` are fine. Leave
  out what PyPI cannot render: Mermaid diagrams, `<picture>`, and images
  by relative path.
{%- endif %}

#### Sub-READMEs

A {% if is_library %}subpackage{% else %}directory{% endif %} whose use outgrows its {% if is_library %}🧩 Modules{% else %}🗂️ Layout{% endif %} entry gets its
own `README.md` next to its code. The root then acts as a hub: the
entry keeps its summary and links to that README, and examples specific
to it move there.{% if not is_library %} A root README without 🗂️ Layout gains one for the
link.{% endif %}

A sub-README is shaped by what it documents (a file format, a family
of APIs, a pipeline step), so it has no fixed sections. It does keep to
these:

- The title names what it covers: {% if is_library %}the import path in backticks,
  as in `` # `{{ package_name }}.sub` ``{% else %}the directory, or a package's import
  path in backticks{% endif %}.
- One or two sentences under the title say what it covers and when to
  reach for it.
- It may go deeper than the root (concepts, worked examples, choosing
  between APIs, pitfalls) but leaves signatures and parameter details
  to docstrings and does not repeat the root.
- Its code examples run, under the same rule as the root's.
- Headings carry no emoji, so their anchors stay plain.
- Once it runs past a couple of screens, it opens with a `## Contents`
  list: a plain bullet list linking each `##` section, never folded into
  `<details>`: a contents list helps only while it is in view.
- Keep `## Contents` in step with the headings: a commit that adds,
  removes, renames, or reorders a `##` section updates the list. An
  anchor is the heading lowercased, spaces turned into hyphens, and
  punctuation other than `-` and `_` dropped:
  `` ## Resolving conflicts: `on_conflict` `` links as
  `#resolving-conflicts-on_conflict`.
- No "back to top" links: GitHub's file view has its own Top button.
{%- if is_library %}
- It may link relatively: only the root README reaches PyPI.
{%- endif %}

### `docs/decisions/`

Record a design decision (a choice between real alternatives that
later work should respect rather than reopen) as an Architecture
Decision Record: one file per decision at
`docs/decisions/NNNN-title-with-dashes.md`, numbered in sequence from
`0001` and never renumbered. Create the directory with the first record.
A rule for how code is written is not a decision record; it goes to
`AGENTS.md`.

Use the [MADR](https://adr.github.io/madr/) shape, kept short:

```markdown
# <Decision, stated as a short sentence>

- Status: accepted
- Date: YYYY-MM-DD

## Context and problem statement

## Considered options

## Decision outcome

## Consequences
```

- Write the record in the commit that implements the decision, or in
  its own `📝` commit when the decision comes first.
- A record is history: once accepted, change it only to fix a factual
  error. When a later decision reverses it, write a new record and set
  the old one's status to `superseded by NNNN`.

### `AGENTS.md`

- When a project-specific convention, command, or pitfall is settled
  (the user states a rule, or a non-obvious workflow is agreed on),
  record it below the `<!-- Add project-specific guidance below. -->`
  marker at the end of this file so later sessions follow it too.
- Leave everything above the marker as is: it is rendered from the
  copier template and refreshed by `copier update`, so local edits there
  turn into merge conflicts. If a template rule does not fit this
  project, add an overriding note below the marker and tell the user.

